1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,114
Today, electricity powers the globe.

2
00:00:04,115 --> 00:00:16,000
1870, the world is driven by human hands, forces, and steam, and precariously lit by open flame and gas.

3
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:24,480
The event that changes the world, Thomas Edison perfects the incandescent vault and lays the foundation for the age of technology.

4
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:29,520
Now, the story of electric light on Modern Models.

5
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:58,560
At 30, Thomas Edison was one of America's best known heroes.

6
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:02,620
The creator of the phonograph, the marvel of the age.

7
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:09,920
Edison lived in Menlo Park, an isolated village in New Jersey.

8
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:16,120
This unimpressive bluster of shacks would become the world's first invention factory.

9
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:25,400
Menlo Park was also the isolated home for Edison's young wife, Mary Stilwell.

10
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:30,340
Mary was a simple young girl Edison met when she worked in one of his factories.

11
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,140
Unprepared for her new life and often left alone as Edison worked around the clock,

12
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,720
Mary would soon be overwhelmed by the growing shadow of her husband's legend.

13
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:48,920
By 1877, creating a legend had taken its toll on Edison, too.

14
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:53,160
For the first time in his life, he was exhausted and sick.

15
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:57,180
He hadn't had a vacation since his marriage six years before.

16
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:00,640
Only after he added the word working to vacation,

17
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:04,520
did he agree to join a group of scientists on their way to Colorado

18
00:02:04,521 --> 00:02:10,720
to study an eclipse of the sun, Mary was left behind in Menlo Park.

19
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:17,680
On the way with boyish enthusiasm, Edison got permission to ride on the cowcatcher

20
00:02:17,681 --> 00:02:21,060
for an uninhibited view of the passing scenery.

21
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:27,600
The Colorado gathering was a distinguished group of university-trained scientists,

22
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:33,380
but Edison, second from the right, with only three months of formal education,

23
00:02:33,381 --> 00:02:36,260
was certainly the most widely known.

24
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:40,600
They were there to study the eclipse,

25
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:43,660
but the talk was also of exciting new developments

26
00:02:43,661 --> 00:02:45,660
in the world of electric illumination.

27
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:49,320
When it was suggested that Edison make this

28
00:02:49,321 --> 00:02:52,520
his next area of experimentation, he was intrigued.

29
00:02:53,620 --> 00:02:55,340
The idea came, he recalled,

30
00:02:55,341 --> 00:02:58,580
just at a time, I wanted to take up something new.

31
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:02,220
As the eclipse turned day into night,

32
00:03:02,221 --> 00:03:09,140
Edison was already thinking of creating a new kind of sun to turn night into day, an electric light.

33
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:12,800
I think Edison was a man who had his eye out for the main chance.

34
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:15,140
I think he could sense what was happening.

35
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:19,960
He'd been involved in the electrical business through his work on telegraphy for a long time.

36
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:27,020
And he essentially saw that there was a revolution on the horizon.

37
00:03:27,021 --> 00:03:35,020
Back in Menlo Park in August 1877, Edison was afire with ideas for a new electric light.

38
00:03:35,021 --> 00:03:40,020
But he was far from the first to tackle the problem.

39
00:03:40,021 --> 00:03:46,020
Edison did not invent the electric light, if you'll excuse the pun, in a vacuum.

40
00:03:46,021 --> 00:03:52,020
Edison was one of dozens of inventors on both sides of the Atlantic doing work on electricity,

41
00:03:52,021 --> 00:03:56,840
electricity, on the electric lighting in general, in particular, and electricity and electric

42
00:03:56,841 --> 00:03:58,720
power in general.

43
00:03:58,721 --> 00:04:05,240
In fact, one survey shows that Edison's light was 23rd on a long list of attempts going

44
00:04:05,241 --> 00:04:10,160
back as far as an English inventor in 1808.

45
00:04:10,161 --> 00:04:14,900
The idea that you could take an electric current and produce illumination goes all the way

46
00:04:14,901 --> 00:04:19,060
back to the first decade or so of the 19th century.

47
00:04:19,061 --> 00:04:23,660
Humphrey Davy, who was one of the first great experimenters with electrical phenomena,

48
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:29,520
demonstrated the two basic forms of electric light, which, say, an arc light,

49
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:35,120
and this alternative form that everybody knew was at least theoretically possible,

50
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:36,940
which was an incandescent light.

51
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:42,820
Arc light is created when electricity jumps the gap between two conductors,

52
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:45,340
creating a continuous blue glow.

53
00:04:45,341 --> 00:04:54,340
Incandescent light occurs when electricity passes through a conductor like a metal filament, creating heat.

54
00:04:54,341 --> 00:04:58,340
As the filament heats up, it glows with light.

55
00:04:58,341 --> 00:05:07,340
A stumbling block for any kind of electric light is an adequate source of electricity.

56
00:05:07,341 --> 00:05:14,340
In 1931, an Englishman, Michael Faraday, created a potential solution to this problem.

57
00:05:14,341 --> 00:05:21,340
The first electromagnetic generator.

58
00:05:21,341 --> 00:05:28,340
By the time Edison began his work in 1877, there already was electrical light.

59
00:05:28,341 --> 00:05:36,340
A year before, arc lights powered by steam dynamos illuminated the Philadelphia Exposition.

60
00:05:36,341 --> 00:05:41,480
In 1877, a whole street in Paris was lit by electricity.

61
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:50,600
Arc illumination worked, but its light was harsh, and it had another serious shortcoming.

62
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:57,820
Arc lamps, as were being used commercially at that time, were operated in series, one after another.

63
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:00,200
So you might have 10 or 15 of them in a row.

64
00:06:00,201 --> 00:06:09,135
Anybody who's familiar with old-fashioned Christmas tree lights in which one bulb burns out and all the rest of them goes out can see the folly of these types of

65
00:06:09,136 --> 00:06:11,300
systems for a domestic lighting system.

66
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:19,120
So Edison had to develop a light that was a lot dimmer, a lot smaller, a lot safer to be used in the home.

67
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:24,400
He believed the way to go was with an incandescent light using the heated wire to do it.

68
00:06:24,401 --> 00:06:34,720
As early as the 1850s, well before Edison, an English druggist, Joseph Swan, was experimenting successfully with an incandescent light bulb.

69
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:37,600
Edison was in a race.

70
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:48,600
Edison hadn't been experimenting with electric light as long as Swan, but he'd been interested in things electrical since he was a bull.

71
00:06:48,601 --> 00:06:54,380
A film biography shows this as well as his lifelong love of practical jokes.

72
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:59,420
Here he attaches a wire to a faucet and gives fellow workers a little shock.

73
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:07,840
In 1876, a little more sophisticated ingenuity produced the electric pen.

74
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:13,560
The forerunner of the modern mimeograph duplicating machine.

75
00:07:13,561 --> 00:07:18,160
But it was a long way from the electric pen to the electric light.

76
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:26,860
Yet even before the real research had begun, Edison brashly announced to the world that he understood the secret of the electric light.

77
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:30,680
On September 13, 1877, he wrote,

78
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:40,640
The object of this invention is to produce light for illuminating purposes by metals heated to incandescence by the passing of electrical current through them.

79
00:07:40,641 --> 00:07:51,840
Not only that, he promised to illuminate a whole neighborhood of New York with an entirely new process all in a matter of months.

80
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:55,280
Edison's announcement made worldwide news.

81
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:59,980
He was confident he discovered the essential concept to do the job.

82
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:05,760
But in fact, he had no detailed idea how he was actually going to make it happen.

83
00:08:05,761 --> 00:08:14,080
In the 18th century, people flocked to demonstrations by professors of electricity.

84
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:19,700
These scientific entertainers used increasingly powerful static electric generators,

85
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:23,640
which when touched, eventually threw one of them across the room.

86
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:27,800
Electricity will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

87
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:35,620
Thomas Edison's announcement that he found the secret of the electric light astonished the public.

88
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:40,760
But to back up his words, he was going to need money.

89
00:08:40,761 --> 00:08:43,760
Lots of it.

90
00:08:43,761 --> 00:08:50,760
In order to get the kind of money that he was talking about, he had to get the attention of a capitalist on Wall Street.

91
00:08:50,761 --> 00:08:56,760
And in particular, I think he was at least vaguely aware of the fact that he had not only to get their attention,

92
00:08:56,761 --> 00:08:59,760
but he had to scare them a little bit.

93
00:08:59,761 --> 00:09:04,760
To say to them, if you don't help me out here, your gas stocks are not going to be worth anything.

94
00:09:04,761 --> 00:09:10,000
anything. Your other investments in similar technologies may go down the tube

95
00:09:10,001 --> 00:09:14,840
and you will feel very foolish because you will have lost the opportunity of a

96
00:09:14,841 --> 00:09:21,520
lifetime to get in on something really big. Wall Street magnates like JP Morgan

97
00:09:21,521 --> 00:09:27,480
heavily invested in gas stocks didn't need to be asked twice. Edison may have

98
00:09:27,481 --> 00:09:31,040
preempted the winner's crown in the race for the electric light but his

99
00:09:31,041 --> 00:09:35,740
His competitors were far from ready to quit.

100
00:09:35,741 --> 00:09:40,800
As this English cartoon shows, he was only one of a big field of inventors with ideas

101
00:09:40,801 --> 00:09:46,760
that disturbed the sleep of international gas magnates.

102
00:09:46,761 --> 00:09:51,460
And Edison was a newcomer.

103
00:09:51,461 --> 00:09:57,040
In England, Joseph Swan was close to success.

104
00:09:57,041 --> 00:10:03,400
to home in New York, Edison faced another challenger, hard-drinking ex-telegrapher William

105
00:10:03,401 --> 00:10:04,101
Sawyer.

106
00:10:04,102 --> 00:10:10,660
With financial support from lawyer Alvin Mann, Sawyer was getting his own Sawyer

107
00:10:10,661 --> 00:10:15,207
Mann electric light ready to glow ahead of Edison.

108
00:10:15,208 --> 00:10:18,480
For the most part, men like Swan and

109
00:10:18,481 --> 00:10:25,989
Sawyer were essentially lone inventors. To many, that was Edison's reputation, too.

110
00:10:25,990 --> 00:10:26,690
But

111
00:10:26,691 --> 00:10:30,680
In fact, at Menlo Park, he was the captain of a powerful team.

112
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:34,620
It was this support that made an enormous difference.

113
00:10:35,540 --> 00:10:41,280
He called his Menlo Park team the boys, or even more affectionately, his muckers.

114
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:46,360
Edison was only 31, but his muckers called him the old man.

115
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:53,820
Edison's closest associate was English-born mechanic Charles Batchelor.

116
00:10:53,821 --> 00:10:59,640
Much of what we know about Edison's early experiments come from Batchelor's detailed

117
00:10:59,641 --> 00:11:03,480
drawings and meticulous notebooks.

118
00:11:03,481 --> 00:11:10,260
Turning these drawings into working machines was the job of master machinist John Kruse.

119
00:11:10,261 --> 00:11:19,040
Kruse was the man who built Edison's first working tin foil talking machine.

120
00:11:19,041 --> 00:11:22,480
A newcomer to the lab was 26-year-old Francis Upton.

121
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:29,480
Unlike most of Edison's muckers, Upton was a mathematician with a university education.

122
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:35,480
It wasn't long before the old man nicknamed him Culture.

123
00:11:36,380 --> 00:11:42,560
Upton would prove invaluable as Edison plunged into the only partially understood complexities of electricity.

124
00:11:42,980 --> 00:11:46,200
Did Edison invent everything by himself? Of course not.

125
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:51,220
One can only imagine these sessions sitting around the organ late at night,

126
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:56,560
you know, having their lunches at midnight brought up from down by the railroad station,

127
00:11:56,680 --> 00:11:59,880
things of this sort, you know, discussing various ideas.

128
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:01,740
I mean, that's the way things happen.

129
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:09,460
With his boys, Edison's Menlo Park Laboratory had come a long way toward achieving his dream,

130
00:12:09,461 --> 00:12:11,240
a perfect workshop.

131
00:12:11,241 --> 00:12:20,240
In 1878, what he hadn't achieved, despite endless hours in the lab, was a workable electric light.

132
00:12:20,241 --> 00:12:30,240
As this early film biography shows, Edison virtually lived in the laboratory, smoking as many as 20 cheap cigars a day.

133
00:12:30,241 --> 00:12:35,240
He worked around the clock, catching catnaps on laboratory benches.

134
00:12:35,241 --> 00:12:49,241
He would wear the same clothes for three or four weeks, go to bed with all his clothes on, including his shoes, not shave, not change his shirt, eat poorly, stay up to

135
00:12:49,552 --> 00:12:54,780
all hours, four or five in the morning, take a 20-minute nap.

136
00:12:55,860 --> 00:12:58,840
His life had an anarchistic aspect to it.

137
00:13:00,420 --> 00:13:04,700
Edison's obsession with his work was devastating to his family life.

138
00:13:04,701 --> 00:13:14,020
In 1879, his wife Mary, long neglected as her legendary husband struggled to solve the mysteries of electricity, was pregnant and depressed.

139
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:20,000
It didn't matter to him what the problem was with the other person, the woman.

140
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:21,680
It didn't matter what her needs were.

141
00:13:21,740 --> 00:13:30,900
Once she started having babies and started having economic cash flow problems and having to run the household without him being there,

142
00:13:30,901 --> 00:13:37,560
then I think the whole relationship went through a tremendous shift, a downshift, as it were,

143
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:39,700
and the romance went out of it completely.

144
00:13:40,600 --> 00:13:45,300
If Edison noticed what was happening to his marriage, he still buried himself in work.

145
00:13:46,060 --> 00:13:51,200
He had discovered that the making of a workable electric light was a lot harder than he'd expected.

146
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:56,380
When you look at Edison's notebooks from 1878,

147
00:13:56,381 --> 00:14:07,380
It's really quite remarkable how you can see the path from cocky confidence to, in a matter of just a few weeks,

148
00:14:07,381 --> 00:14:11,380
oh, this isn't working, let's try all of these other things.

149
00:14:11,381 --> 00:14:19,380
And so, in fact, by the end of 1878, after three or four months of experimentation, Edison is beginning to sweat.

150
00:14:19,381 --> 00:14:27,480
An important goal in creating a workable incandescent lamp was to find a filament that would keep glowing and not burn up or melt.

151
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:35,100
Like others before him, Edison started with platinum, a metal with a high melting point.

152
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:37,940
But platinum was rare and expensive.

153
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:47,480
Since the experiments of Humphrey Davy in the early 1800s, it was known that one way to make a filament last was to put it in a vacuum.

154
00:14:48,460 --> 00:14:52,400
Without oxygen to encourage combustion, the filament would glow longer.

155
00:14:55,280 --> 00:14:59,880
To respond to this problem, a new mucker from Germany was added to the team.

156
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:05,080
Ludwig Baum was cocky and talented and a master glass blower.

157
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:13,420
His ability to turn out perfect vacuum glassware again and again was a crucial contribution to Edison's work.

158
00:15:13,421 --> 00:15:22,900
Finally, there was the centuries-old problem of an adequate source of electricity to power the bulb.

159
00:15:25,060 --> 00:15:31,740
Edison took advantage of the fact that there had been important improvements in electrical generators since Michael Faraday's time.

160
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:43,000
Edison added his own contribution to the field with the development of a device that his boys thought looked like a woman on her back with both legs in the air.

161
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:50,420
They nicknamed the new electrical generator the long-legged Mary Ann.

162
00:15:50,421 --> 00:15:57,420
More Victorian sensibilities later changed it to the long-wasted Mary Ann.

163
00:15:57,421 --> 00:16:02,420
By the middle of 1879, Edison had made some progress,

164
00:16:02,421 --> 00:16:08,420
but much of the scientific establishment was convinced the odds of success were against him.

165
00:16:08,421 --> 00:16:11,420
He was called a self-promoting humbug,

166
00:16:11,421 --> 00:16:16,300
and his declarations of imminent success were mocked as sheer nonsense.

167
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,820
Edison's investors were getting anxious, too.

168
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:28,580
By March 1879, Edison couldn't put them off any longer.

169
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:33,840
He reluctantly set up a demonstration of progress on the miracle he promised.

170
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:36,820
It would be a memorable occasion.

171
00:16:36,821 --> 00:16:40,003
Well, in this March visit, Edison is all set up.

172
00:16:40,004 --> 00:16:50,280
He's got his lamps, he's got the generator hooked up, and the lights come up and they look very nice, but all of a sudden they start going out, one by one.

173
00:16:50,720 --> 00:16:57,280
And pretty soon, there they are in the dark, wondering if, in fact, Edison has the way to solve the problem.

174
00:16:57,281 --> 00:17:10,040
For Edison, the pressure was on, and he knew he wasn't the only inventor searching for a solution.

175
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:18,620
The career of British inventor Sir Joseph Swan paralleled Edison's in many areas.

176
00:17:19,700 --> 00:17:24,740
Not only did Swan invent an incandescent light, he also held many patents in photography.

177
00:17:24,741 --> 00:17:29,740
Electricity will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

178
00:17:29,741 --> 00:17:36,740
All during 1879, Thomas Edison was working under the greatest pressure of his life.

179
00:17:36,741 --> 00:17:42,740
If you look at the technical journals, you'll see that there are a host of inventors who are announcing breakthroughs,

180
00:17:42,741 --> 00:17:47,740
that are announcing that they are just on the edge of solving the problem of the electric light.

181
00:17:47,741 --> 00:17:56,180
And Edison is, to a certain extent, just one of its host of inventors racing to the goal of a successful electric light.

182
00:17:56,740 --> 00:18:04,540
Unlike most of his competitors, from the beginning Edison thought of creating a system of lights as well as inventing a single lamp.

183
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:14,620
And with a brilliant stroke of insight as early as 1877, he knew what the basic characteristics of that system needed to be.

184
00:18:14,621 --> 00:18:23,620
Edison competitors such as William Sawyer were working with lamps that use low resistance electrical conductors.

185
00:18:23,621 --> 00:18:31,620
When an electrical conductor resists the flow of electricity, this electrical friction generates heat.

186
00:18:31,621 --> 00:18:37,620
This heat makes the filament in an electric light glow.

187
00:18:37,621 --> 00:18:47,140
With a low resistance conductor, there is less electrical friction, so it takes more electricity to build up the heat needed for light.

188
00:18:48,760 --> 00:18:51,500
More electricity means thicker conductors.

189
00:18:53,700 --> 00:19:00,580
Thicker conductors add excess heat, and more important, additional cost to an electrical system.

190
00:19:00,581 --> 00:19:09,580
High resistance conductors with greater electric friction need less electricity to produce the heat for light.

191
00:19:10,580 --> 00:19:13,640
This means they can be thinner and cheaper.

192
00:19:15,360 --> 00:19:18,720
Edison was convinced his high resistance concept was correct.

193
00:19:19,340 --> 00:19:23,100
But without a workable electric light, it was nothing but a clever theory.

194
00:19:24,680 --> 00:19:29,540
As 1879 dragged on, Edison suffered the pains of hell.

195
00:19:30,580 --> 00:19:34,660
He was almost blind from watching one filament after another fail.

196
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:38,280
He could only sleep with the help of a large dose of morphine.

197
00:19:38,760 --> 00:19:41,000
But he and his muckers kept going.

198
00:19:42,240 --> 00:19:46,620
You could say that he was inspired by his sense of competition constantly,

199
00:19:47,260 --> 00:19:53,940
regardless of the reality of the competition that he either imagined was really there or not.

200
00:19:53,941 --> 00:19:56,900
It was always there in his mind.

201
00:19:56,901 --> 00:20:05,820
Groping for an answer, Edison would often take breaks and pound out a tune with two fingers on an organ he'd installed in the lab.

202
00:20:06,540 --> 00:20:09,320
There were so many problems that needed to be solved.

203
00:20:10,340 --> 00:20:16,120
Achieving an adequate vacuum inside the lamp's bulb was critical to achieving a long-lasting filament.

204
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:18,500
It was a major challenge.

205
00:20:18,501 --> 00:20:29,413
The Edison bulb has to have a vacuum in it that is so very good that if one molecule of gas is moving through space in that vacuum, it will not collide with another

206
00:20:29,414 --> 00:20:32,540
molecule of gas for something like three miles.

207
00:20:33,280 --> 00:20:37,340
So it is an extraordinarily high vacuum that is necessary.

208
00:20:37,900 --> 00:20:46,500
During the summer of 1879, the arrival of the most advanced vacuum pump of its day gave a critical boost to Edison's work.

209
00:20:46,501 --> 00:20:55,800
Even greater impetus came from Edison's decision to switch his filament from expensive platinum to cheaper, more high-resistance carbon.

210
00:20:57,640 --> 00:21:07,300
Despite the fact that competitors like Swan were using carbon, Edison resisted since carbon was hard to make into the thin filament his high-resistance lamp needed.

211
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:11,160
All that remained was the conclusive test.

212
00:21:11,161 --> 00:21:20,640
The whole question of the development of the electric lamp is one in which I think that there were obviously were flashes of inspiration along the way.

213
00:21:21,140 --> 00:21:24,100
Any inventor works that way. I don't care who it is.

214
00:21:24,460 --> 00:21:28,380
But it is plain, simple, dogged, hard work.

215
00:21:29,460 --> 00:21:35,800
Searching for a way to make a thin carbon filament, Edison decided to try standard sewing thread.

216
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:39,340
Slowly baked until it was charred.

217
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:45,560
It was just one more experiment.

218
00:21:45,561 --> 00:21:52,200
It had been 15 months since Edison declared he had the secret of the electric light.

219
00:21:52,201 --> 00:21:57,620
15 months of trial and error, with public expectations growing,

220
00:21:57,621 --> 00:22:01,720
and the press eager for headlines.

221
00:22:01,721 --> 00:22:05,000
The competitive environment in the 1870s was simple.

222
00:22:05,001 --> 00:22:09,340
It was that within a year or two, somebody was going to invent a successful incandescent

223
00:22:09,341 --> 00:22:10,913
electric light bulb.

224
00:22:10,914 --> 00:22:16,260
Edison and his muckers were deep into unexplored territory and there

225
00:22:16,261 --> 00:22:21,221
was no light in sight until October 21st, 1879.

226
00:22:21,222 --> 00:22:25,760
On that date, Edison tried his new filament

227
00:22:25,761 --> 00:22:28,786
using carbonized sewing thread.

228
00:22:28,787 --> 00:22:33,960
He puts in the current through this cotton thread and

229
00:22:33,961 --> 00:22:38,100
it glows and it continues to glow

230
00:22:38,101 --> 00:22:41,620
and it continues to glow and he increases the current

231
00:22:41,621 --> 00:22:45,960
so that it goes from a yellow heat to an orange

232
00:22:45,961 --> 00:22:50,860
to something of an almost white heat

233
00:22:50,861 --> 00:22:53,720
and the thing continues to glow

234
00:22:53,721 --> 00:22:57,420
and it continues to glow for a whole series of hours

235
00:22:57,421 --> 00:23:01,400
into the night and Edison and his crew begin

236
00:23:01,401 --> 00:23:06,880
gathering around this, what had started out, eject another one of the experimental labs,

237
00:23:07,160 --> 00:23:09,440
and they say, we've got it.

238
00:23:10,840 --> 00:23:14,620
The carbonized thread lasted for 13 hours.

239
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:17,540
Good, but not good enough.

240
00:23:20,440 --> 00:23:23,640
Edison launched an exhaustive search for something better.

241
00:23:24,420 --> 00:23:30,740
He and his muckers tested fish lines, celluloid, coconut fiber, paper, playing cards, even human hair.

242
00:23:31,400 --> 00:23:33,480
Bamboo was an early success.

243
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:39,260
With research still in progress on November 4th, 1879,

244
00:23:40,060 --> 00:23:44,500
Edison made another patent application for his improved incandescent light.

245
00:23:45,140 --> 00:23:49,300
The same day, he began preparations for a full-scale demonstration.

246
00:23:50,380 --> 00:23:55,140
Edison, the inventor, was about to become Edison, the master publicist.

247
00:23:55,560 --> 00:23:58,800
The press is a crucial part of not just Edison's image,

248
00:23:58,801 --> 00:24:02,180
but the thing that allows him to become Edison.

249
00:24:03,500 --> 00:24:06,280
It's not just the fact that he has this marvelous invention,

250
00:24:06,440 --> 00:24:09,460
but it's the fact that people become interested in him,

251
00:24:09,940 --> 00:24:13,000
and he begins to become this person who can do anything.

252
00:24:14,560 --> 00:24:19,480
Newspaper articles outdid each other to extol Edison's latest triumph,

253
00:24:20,080 --> 00:24:23,020
well before the first bulb was turned on in public.

254
00:24:23,021 --> 00:24:35,020
With a typical sense of the dramatic, Edison scheduled a debut of the new era of the electric light for New Year's Eve 1879.

255
00:24:35,021 --> 00:24:43,020
Wires were strung from the lab and bulbs placed along pathways and in surrounding buildings,

256
00:24:43,021 --> 00:24:49,020
including the nearby boarding house where many of Edison's lab workers lived.

257
00:24:49,021 --> 00:24:56,020
When the big day came, 3,000 people swarmed over little Menlo Park.

258
00:24:56,021 --> 00:25:07,352
He got all the New York journalists, the tabloid writers and the weekly writers, and he took them up into the lab and gave them champagne and oysters and took them

259
00:25:07,353 --> 00:25:09,020
around this little tour.

260
00:25:09,021 --> 00:25:20,040
At a certain point as dusk was falling, he flicked a switch and a hundred light bulbs went on all over Menlo Park on these sort of wooden stanchions.

261
00:25:21,300 --> 00:25:26,140
There was a film of snow on the ground and there was a layer of frost on all the trees.

262
00:25:26,680 --> 00:25:31,580
And he created this really like a winter wonderland in sort of an instantaneous way like that.

263
00:25:31,840 --> 00:25:33,260
And people just went crazy.

264
00:25:33,261 --> 00:25:44,760
A drunken William Sawyer, Edison's New York competitor, was reported staggering through the astonished crowd, declaring he was the true inventor of the electric light.

265
00:25:45,220 --> 00:25:46,180
But no one listened.

266
00:25:46,980 --> 00:25:50,300
The Wizard of Menlo Park had created the invention of the century.

267
00:25:51,140 --> 00:25:52,480
Well, almost.

268
00:25:53,800 --> 00:25:57,720
A few workable electric lights were only a start.

269
00:25:58,720 --> 00:26:02,380
What about that promise to illuminate New York?

270
00:26:03,260 --> 00:26:12,240
Edison used carbonized strips of bamboo for his first filaments in 1880.

271
00:26:12,940 --> 00:26:17,460
Today's metal of choice, tungsten, wasn't available until 1912.

272
00:26:18,960 --> 00:26:22,680
Electricity will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

273
00:26:22,681 --> 00:26:33,700
By the end of January 1888, Edison's light had burned for 550 hours.

274
00:26:34,200 --> 00:26:38,440
The popular press declared him the winner of the race for the electric light.

275
00:26:39,220 --> 00:26:41,480
But his competitors weren't giving up.

276
00:26:42,040 --> 00:26:44,220
Even so, they had some catching up to do.

277
00:26:44,221 --> 00:26:54,480
William Sawyer had proposed a system of lighting in a house that shows, you know, essentially light bulbs screwed into sockets on chandeliers.

278
00:26:55,440 --> 00:27:08,564
But the important fact is, if you look at a Sawyer man lamp from 1878 or 1879, you find it an impossibly complex, large, heavy device that runs with a low resistance

279
00:27:08,565 --> 00:27:09,280
filament.

280
00:27:09,281 --> 00:27:16,280
Joseph Swan, Edison's English competitor, was also working with a low resistance lamp.

281
00:27:16,281 --> 00:27:19,280
But he was more than close to the finish line.

282
00:27:19,281 --> 00:27:31,546
The plain and simple fact remains that a successful Swan electric lamp, incandescent lamp, was demonstrated in Newcastle in the latter part of 1878, fully a year ahead

283
00:27:31,547 --> 00:27:32,280
of Edison.

284
00:27:32,281 --> 00:27:33,049
of Edison.

285
00:27:33,050 --> 00:27:39,280
Despite their early successes to historians, what made Sawyer and Swan runners-up

286
00:27:40,080 --> 00:27:44,159
was the lack of a lamp that worked in a large-scale system.

287
00:27:44,160 --> 00:27:46,440
But in 1880, Edison didn't have a

288
00:27:46,441 --> 00:27:50,475
system either. He needed one, and it had to work.

289
00:27:50,476 --> 00:27:53,440
Everything is so new, he wrote, that

290
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:57,728
each step is in the dark.

291
00:27:57,729 --> 00:28:02,220
I have to make the dynamos, the lamps, the conductors, and attend

292
00:28:02,221 --> 00:28:06,600
to a thousand details the world has never heard of.

293
00:28:06,601 --> 00:28:12,180
To be successful, an electric light not only had to be as bright as a gas lamp,

294
00:28:12,181 --> 00:28:15,360
it had to be as economical and reliable.

295
00:28:15,361 --> 00:28:18,580
And most important, it needed to be mass produced

296
00:28:18,581 --> 00:28:22,760
to work in a large interconnected system.

297
00:28:22,761 --> 00:28:26,560
Early on, Edison knew how such a system would be laid out.

298
00:28:26,561 --> 00:28:29,620
He would subdivide the light, like gas pipes.

299
00:28:29,621 --> 00:28:32,940
There would be a main line with feeders to individual lamps.

300
00:28:35,220 --> 00:28:39,080
This would allow each lamp to be controlled independently.

301
00:28:40,800 --> 00:28:43,940
This was the system. What remained was to build it.

302
00:28:45,900 --> 00:28:53,360
Edison had chosen a one-quarter mile district of lower Manhattan around Pearl Street for his first real-world installation.

303
00:28:53,361 --> 00:29:02,360
It wasn't accidental that Pearl Street was near the headquarters of many of his major Wall Street financiers.

304
00:29:02,361 --> 00:29:14,163
He worked feverishly to be able to put together enough of his system so that he can convince a capitalist not only has their money been well invested, but more

305
00:29:14,164 --> 00:29:19,360
importantly for him at this point, that more money is worth investing.

306
00:29:19,361 --> 00:29:20,315
investing.

307
00:29:20,316 --> 00:29:27,760
The electric light would change the world and the changes began at Menlo Park.

308
00:29:28,720 --> 00:29:32,720
When Edison began his research, he had 15 employees.

309
00:29:35,160 --> 00:29:39,760
By the time he made his first public demonstration, the staff had jumped to 64.

310
00:29:41,740 --> 00:29:48,960
By the end of 1880, it would be 220. The era of close-knit camaraderie was over.

311
00:29:49,360 --> 00:29:56,360
It was a change that would have lasting impact on both Edison the man as well as his company.

312
00:29:56,361 --> 00:30:02,360
Edison's Menlo Park machine shop had produced the first hardware for the electric light.

313
00:30:02,361 --> 00:30:08,360
But full-scale manufacturing demanded larger facilities.

314
00:30:08,361 --> 00:30:12,360
Edison moved his electricity operation to New York.

315
00:30:12,361 --> 00:30:19,160
A simpler way of working and the tools that made the first phonograph were left behind.

316
00:30:21,700 --> 00:30:25,440
Work began at Pearl Street as anxious investors looked on.

317
00:30:25,900 --> 00:30:27,660
The job would take two long years.

318
00:30:29,540 --> 00:30:32,380
Edison needed to build more and better generators.

319
00:30:33,500 --> 00:30:39,840
Lay miles of underground wire and create and install switches, fixtures and meters.

320
00:30:42,360 --> 00:30:50,120
But before he could start, he needed to woo local politicians for permission to dig up city streets.

321
00:30:52,680 --> 00:30:57,260
A lavish dinner with free-flowing champagne did the trick.

322
00:30:58,040 --> 00:31:01,480
The politicians left convinced the wizard was one of them.

323
00:31:02,340 --> 00:31:04,280
In a way, he was.

324
00:31:04,940 --> 00:31:09,080
On September 4, 1882, the big day finally came.

325
00:31:09,081 --> 00:31:15,510
Staying close to his financiers, Edison went to the Wall Street offices of J.P.

326
00:31:15,511 --> 00:31:16,211
Morgan.

327
00:31:16,212 --> 00:31:20,080
There, he turned a switch.

328
00:31:20,081 --> 00:31:24,080
Light bulbs began to glow.

329
00:31:24,081 --> 00:31:28,080
Edison's response was quiet triumph.

330
00:31:28,081 --> 00:31:31,080
I've accomplished all I have promised.

331
00:31:31,081 --> 00:31:33,080
He'd beaten the competition.

332
00:31:33,081 --> 00:31:36,080
But an even bigger fight lay ahead.

333
00:31:36,081 --> 00:31:41,580
After the switches were turned on at Pearl Street, progress was steady.

334
00:31:41,581 --> 00:31:46,480
The system opened with 2,323 lamps in 1882.

335
00:31:46,481 --> 00:31:52,680
Two years later, there were 11,272 lamps in 500 premises.

336
00:31:52,681 --> 00:31:58,680
A year later in 1885, there were 250,000 lamps in use in the United States.

337
00:31:58,681 --> 00:32:04,580
One of Edison's first lessons was don't invent anything that nobody else wants.

338
00:32:04,581 --> 00:32:10,840
He understood to be a successful inventor, he had to create things for which there was a market.

339
00:32:11,900 --> 00:32:15,900
Edison would soon be called the Napoleon of invention.

340
00:32:16,760 --> 00:32:21,040
He was pleased to look the part, but his leadership was challenged from the beginning.

341
00:32:21,940 --> 00:32:26,180
The ink on his patents was hardly dry before they were contested in court.

342
00:32:28,160 --> 00:32:32,380
Competitors improved, modified, and stole his ideas.

343
00:32:33,260 --> 00:32:42,220
In the United States, a partnership led by electrical entrepreneur Ella Hugh Thompson soon had a sizable share of the electricity business.

344
00:32:43,960 --> 00:32:52,600
In England, Joseph Swan's work was advanced enough that Edison agreed to join him in a partnership to avoid a long and costly patent battle.

345
00:32:55,020 --> 00:32:59,600
Their company, a British monopoly for a decade, was called Ediswan.

346
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:03,360
Edison made sure his name came first.

347
00:33:05,780 --> 00:33:12,100
In America, Edison attempted to dominate the competition with his Edison General Electric Company.

348
00:33:13,160 --> 00:33:17,680
But even William Sawyer was a formidable courtroom adversary.

349
00:33:19,580 --> 00:33:22,900
In fact, at one time, during a long legal struggle,

350
00:33:23,820 --> 00:33:27,280
Sawyer was declared the true inventor of the incandescent lamp,

351
00:33:27,281 --> 00:33:29,980
An appeal returned the honor to Edison.

352
00:33:31,240 --> 00:33:34,960
The exhausting battle soured Edison on the patent system.

353
00:33:35,520 --> 00:33:40,860
Later he wrote, my electric light inventions have brought me no profits, only 40 years of litigation.

354
00:33:41,780 --> 00:33:50,260
In the midst of it all, in 1884, mentally and physically unstable, Edison's wife, Mary, died.

355
00:33:51,280 --> 00:33:53,660
Edison deeply mourned Mary's death.

356
00:33:54,520 --> 00:34:00,660
In his way, he loved her and their three children, but in the end, he never left enough time for them.

357
00:34:02,320 --> 00:34:07,940
Despite his legend, Edison wasn't everything, and one thing he wasn't was a family man.

358
00:34:09,100 --> 00:34:16,460
With a determination as methodical as the search for the perfect filament, Edison was soon looking for another wife.

359
00:34:17,900 --> 00:34:20,360
Within weeks after Mary's death, he met her.

360
00:34:21,400 --> 00:34:22,420
Mina Miller.

361
00:34:23,660 --> 00:34:25,600
The educated daughter of a millionaire,

362
00:34:25,940 --> 00:34:29,360
Minor was better prepared to deal with Edison's eccentricities.

363
00:34:29,920 --> 00:34:31,540
They married in 1886.

364
00:34:32,240 --> 00:34:34,500
Soon, Edison had three more children.

365
00:34:35,200 --> 00:34:37,360
The marriage would last the rest of his life,

366
00:34:37,520 --> 00:34:40,160
but Edison still had little time for his family.

367
00:34:41,140 --> 00:34:42,820
His mind was on work.

368
00:34:42,821 --> 00:34:47,940
In 1887, he moved his lab to a ten times larger complex

369
00:34:47,941 --> 00:34:50,340
built in West Orange, New Jersey.

370
00:34:50,341 --> 00:35:02,080
For Mina and his family, he bought Glenmont, a grand mansion nearby, but most of his time was still spent with the machines and chemicals at the lab.

371
00:35:02,800 --> 00:35:05,372
It was a place where he felt he had control.

372
00:35:05,373 --> 00:35:08,120
The outside world, however, was another matter.

373
00:35:08,940 --> 00:35:15,740
By 1888, Edison faced a technological war that threatened the foundations of his electric light empire.

374
00:35:20,340 --> 00:35:28,020
The Edison General Electric System was based on direct current, electricity that travels in just one direction.

375
00:35:29,140 --> 00:35:37,900
In 1888, Nikola Tesla, a brilliant scientist who worked briefly for Edison, developed a higher capacity alternating current system.

376
00:35:39,020 --> 00:35:45,300
Tesla sold his patents to industrialist George Westinghouse and battle lines against Edison were drawn.

377
00:35:45,301 --> 00:35:52,300
Direct current or DC systems are limited in their range and capacity.

378
00:35:52,301 --> 00:36:00,300
Alternating current, AC, uses much more powerful generators and was better suited for extensive systems.

379
00:36:00,301 --> 00:36:10,300
By 1895, Westinghouse's AC generators were harnessing the enormous power of Niagara Falls.

380
00:36:10,301 --> 00:36:15,300
Edison vehemently refused to accept AC.

381
00:36:15,301 --> 00:36:21,300
The use of alternating current is unworthy of practical men, he said.

382
00:36:21,301 --> 00:36:25,300
Such stubbornness kept him going during the search for the electric light.

383
00:36:25,301 --> 00:36:28,300
Now it led to short-sightedness.

384
00:36:28,301 --> 00:36:32,300
The idea that Edison would make initial breakthroughs

385
00:36:32,301 --> 00:36:36,300
and sometimes develop his inventions up to a particular point,

386
00:36:36,301 --> 00:36:41,500
but then not be able to make yet another leap into a new kind of a technology,

387
00:36:41,501 --> 00:36:47,880
such as the shift from direct current to alternating current as a dominant electrical system,

388
00:36:48,100 --> 00:36:56,120
I think indicates Edison's sense that he could break through, create the best way of doing something,

389
00:36:56,420 --> 00:37:01,960
and then his own egotistical refusal to see that in fact someone could better him,

390
00:37:02,180 --> 00:37:04,820
or that there was a better way than Edison's way.

391
00:37:05,720 --> 00:37:11,915
With A.C. systems growing in popularity, Edison fought back hard, arguing that A.C.

392
00:37:11,916 --> 00:37:12,960
was dangerous.

393
00:37:13,140 --> 00:37:17,900
There was a lot of dirty pool played by the Edison interests, and I want to emphasize this.

394
00:37:17,960 --> 00:37:20,160
The Edison interests more than Thomas Edison.

395
00:37:21,020 --> 00:37:29,240
In trying to prevent alternating current from becoming important, there was a man named Brown who went around electrocuting dogs.

396
00:37:29,241 --> 00:37:36,240
Such lethal demonstrations were designed to convince consumers that AC was deadly.

397
00:37:36,241 --> 00:37:42,240
It didn't take long for electrical entrepreneurs to move from dog to man.

398
00:37:42,241 --> 00:37:46,240
The result was the electric chair.

399
00:37:46,241 --> 00:37:49,240
It used powerful AC current.

400
00:37:49,241 --> 00:37:58,240
Looking to score points, Edison representatives suggested changing the phrase to electrocute a criminal to Westinghouse him.

401
00:37:58,241 --> 00:38:00,540
The term didn't stick.

402
00:38:02,280 --> 00:38:07,280
But in 1882, there was an even more important name change for Edison.

403
00:38:08,460 --> 00:38:14,720
Ignoring him, Wall Street financiers arranged a merger between one of Edison's largest competitors,

404
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:18,560
Ella Hugh Thompson's company and Edison's General Electric.

405
00:38:19,320 --> 00:38:21,900
The new company was called General Electric.

406
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:24,780
Edison's name had been left off.

407
00:38:24,781 --> 00:38:36,664
By the beginning of the 1890s, it's really capitalists who are in charge, managers who are making decisions about the direction that the electrical industry will go

408
00:38:36,665 --> 00:38:40,360
in, and Edison is really very much on the sideline.

409
00:38:41,400 --> 00:38:45,340
Edison was very hurt by the removal of his name from the company.

410
00:38:45,341 --> 00:38:50,340
Edison eventually resigned from the board of General Electric.

411
00:38:50,341 --> 00:38:55,340
His company continued to create and manufacture electrical appliances and machines,

412
00:38:55,341 --> 00:39:00,340
but in a way, he had been overwhelmed by the revolution he'd launched.

413
00:39:00,341 --> 00:39:03,340
Corporate America was born during Edison's lifetime,

414
00:39:03,341 --> 00:39:07,340
and he was dragged kicking and screaming into that world,

415
00:39:07,341 --> 00:39:09,340
and it was not a world of his making.

416
00:39:09,341 --> 00:39:14,340
He had to fit himself into that world, and I believe that he failed.

417
00:39:14,341 --> 00:39:15,041
failed.

418
00:39:15,042 --> 00:39:21,980
By the 1890s, there were single generators capable of producing the power of Edison's entire

419
00:39:21,981 --> 00:39:23,460
Pearl Street system.

420
00:39:23,461 --> 00:39:28,640
Not only neighborhoods, but complete cities and regions were wired up,

421
00:39:28,860 --> 00:39:31,987
all controlled by giant corporate utilities.

422
00:39:31,988 --> 00:39:35,540
It would be almost 20 years before Edison returned

423
00:39:35,541 --> 00:39:39,314
to General Electric's Schenectady, New York headquarters.

424
00:39:39,315 --> 00:39:41,300
In 1922, he was given the tour

425
00:39:41,301 --> 00:39:44,840
that showed how far the electric light had come since Menlo Park.

426
00:39:45,460 --> 00:39:48,660
When he saw how the latest mass-produced tungsten filaments were made,

427
00:39:48,980 --> 00:39:53,060
he could only shake his head and reminisce about the pains of hell he went through

428
00:39:53,061 --> 00:39:55,020
just to find a filament that worked.

429
00:39:55,940 --> 00:40:00,700
Now, after years of litigation, he used terms like practical and electric light system

430
00:40:00,701 --> 00:40:03,720
to qualify and redefine his achievement.

431
00:40:03,721 --> 00:40:14,240
The filament of the first practical incandescent lamp which I invented was a carbonized cotton

432
00:40:14,241 --> 00:40:20,170
thread. In the search for a better filament, I carbonized everything lightly.

433
00:40:20,171 --> 00:40:20,940
Carbonized

434
00:40:20,941 --> 00:40:27,020
paper horseshoe lamps were used in the first public demonstration of the electric lighting

435
00:40:27,021 --> 00:40:31,220
system in Meadow Park on December 31st, 1879.

436
00:40:32,360 --> 00:40:39,320
By 1922, there was a new generation of academically trained electrical geniuses like Charles Steinmetz.

437
00:40:40,880 --> 00:40:46,020
When Edison was asked why he and Steinmetz were friends, the wizard replied,

438
00:40:46,820 --> 00:40:49,980
when he was with me, he never talked about mathematics.

439
00:40:50,840 --> 00:40:55,700
With the turn of the century, America and the world had entered the age of electricity.

440
00:40:55,701 --> 00:41:00,600
It was an era created by generations of scientists and researchers.

441
00:41:01,560 --> 00:41:05,700
But to many, one man, Thomas Edison, made it all possible.

442
00:41:07,500 --> 00:41:11,040
Edison was a living legend, seemingly ageless.

443
00:41:11,560 --> 00:41:22,160
He joined a group of distinguished friends, Henry Ford, tire magnate Harvey Firestone, and naturalist John Burroughs, on a series of well-publicized camping trips.

444
00:41:22,161 --> 00:41:29,160
Far from an outdoors man, Edison declared, the only thing I use my body for is to carry my brain around.

445
00:41:29,161 --> 00:41:40,160
As a monument to the Edison legend, Henry Ford rebuilt Edison's Menlo Park Laboratory in Dearborn, Michigan.

446
00:41:40,161 --> 00:41:51,160
Accompanied by his wife, Mina, Edison, old and weak, toured the site and left his famous signature, written for the ages, in a slab of concrete.

447
00:41:52,160 --> 00:41:57,160
Thomas Edison was one of the first 20th century superstars.

448
00:41:57,161 --> 00:42:03,160
Always people have been famous for doing things, and that's how Edison earned his fame and his celebrity.

449
00:42:03,161 --> 00:42:08,160
But after a period he became Edison, and newspaper men came to him merely because he was Edison.

450
00:42:08,161 --> 00:42:10,002
You didn't even have to have anything new.

451
00:42:10,003 --> 00:42:13,160
They wanted his pronouncements on almost any subject you could think of.

452
00:42:13,161 --> 00:42:16,160
What do you think of the Einstein theory?

453
00:42:16,161 --> 00:42:22,000
I don't think anything of Einstein theory because I can't understand it.

454
00:42:23,200 --> 00:42:32,120
In 1929, the new Menlo Park was the site of the 50th anniversary celebration of the first electric light system.

455
00:42:33,240 --> 00:42:38,080
Guests included President Hoover, Orville Wright, and Madame Curie.

456
00:42:38,860 --> 00:42:45,580
Although electrical technology had followed the path of alternating current away from Edison's first direct current system,

457
00:42:46,160 --> 00:42:52,440
the 82-year-old Wizard of Menlo Park was hailed as the father of the electrical age.

458
00:42:53,340 --> 00:42:55,320
It was a bittersweet moment.

459
00:42:56,480 --> 00:43:02,120
Three decades before, in 1900, disappointed when General Electric left his name off the company,

460
00:43:02,740 --> 00:43:06,340
Edison had brashly declared, electric lights are too old for me.

461
00:43:06,660 --> 00:43:11,620
He had other worlds to conquer, and it wasn't typical Edison bravado.

462
00:43:12,540 --> 00:43:15,820
Already, the West Orange Lab had launched another revolution.

463
00:43:16,160 --> 00:43:21,160
motion pictures.

464
00:43:21,161 --> 00:43:31,160
Even so, it would be hard to match the glow that lit up Manlo Park in 1879.

465
00:43:31,161 --> 00:43:36,160
I think Edison's electric light, the electric light that emerged out of the late 19th century,

466
00:43:36,161 --> 00:43:39,160
is one of the greatest inventions in history.

467
00:43:39,161 --> 00:43:50,140
It, along with a very small, small handful of invention, remade the world in which people lived and worked and played and died.

468
00:43:51,280 --> 00:43:59,560
The electric light was the kind of invention that could reshape the way in which people looked at the world and at the possibilities in the world.

469
00:44:02,860 --> 00:44:06,860
Such a vision wasn't too large for Thomas Edison.

470
00:44:06,861 --> 00:44:11,020
in 1878 with typical outspoken self-assurance.

471
00:44:11,720 --> 00:44:13,440
Before his light was even born,

472
00:44:13,980 --> 00:44:14,680
he wrote,

473
00:44:14,681 --> 00:44:16,860
The electric light is the light of the future,

474
00:44:17,560 --> 00:44:19,220
and it will be my light.

